// The per-OS-thread identity token (#1650) — `runtime/root/`'s `threadlocal var
// g_mutator` expressed as a KEY rather than as storage.
//
// `gcworld.bit` needs to answer one question at every door into the collector:
// WHICH OS THREAD IS ASKING. A `threadlocal` would answer it, which this
// port cannot have — `@threadlocal` (§11.11) is implemented on ELF but refused
// at emission for Mach-O, and `sched.bit` documents the same wall. The port's
// stand-in was a module-level `let curMut`, i.e. ONE process-wide cell, and that
// is #1650: the second Bit thread read the first thread's slot, concluded it was
// already registered, and was then invisible to both the rendezvous and the root
// scan. It mutated straight through the mark and its function-value cells were
// swept out from under it.
//
// A threadlocal is not needed to key a registry — only a per-thread VALUE that
// two different threads can never share. That is exactly what a thread pointer
// is, and it has three properties nothing stack-derived has:
//
//   1. IT IS EXACT. A stack-derived token (an `sp` granule, a landmark plus a
//      fudge distance, a worker-block range) is an approximation of "which
//      region is this `sp` in", and an approximation that shifts hands ONE
//      thread TWO slots. A thread holding two slots is a thread the rendezvous
//      waits for forever; that was measured as a hang, not deduced.
//   2. IT IS THE SAME ON EITHER STACK. A green task runs on a 64KiB task stack
//      and a worker's own loop runs on its 16MiB OS-thread stack; the safepoint
//      must resolve BOTH to one slot. A register does not care which stack is
//      live, so the two cases need no reconciliation at all.
//   3. IT NEEDS NO COOPERATION. No registration, no bind, no scheduler seam, no
//      ordering against thread creation. It is readable from the first
//      instruction a thread executes.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY ONE `asm` BLOCK COVERS BOTH OPERATING SYSTEMS, WITH NO PLATFORM SEAM
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `sched.bit` writes the seam off ("reading the thread pointer out of
// TPIDRRO_EL0 needs a platform seam too — Linux and Darwin use different system
// registers on the same architecture"). The register split is real; the SEAM is
// not, because on each operating system the other register is not a thread
// pointer at all and says so at run time. MEASURED on hardware, three pthreads
// each, not read off the ARM ARM:
//
//   aarch64-macos   TPIDRRO_EL0 = 1f1c9de60 / 16aeb70e0 / 16af430e0  per-thread
//                   TPIDR_EL0   = 2 / 1 / 2                          THE CPU ID
//   aarch64-linux   TPIDRRO_EL0 = 0 / 0 / 0                          unused
//                   TPIDR_EL0   = 420710 / ffff849c4fe8 / ffff849a1fe8  per-thread
//
// TPIDR_EL0 being a small NONZERO integer on Darwin is the trap in that table:
// it is why the probe order is TPIDRRO_EL0 first and TPIDR_EL0 second, and why
// the reverse order — the one that reads more natural, since TPIDR_EL0 is the
// architectural TLS register — silently hands every thread on a 2-core Mac one
// of two tokens. Linux zeroes TPIDRRO_EL0 for 64-bit tasks, so the first probe
// falls through there and the second answers.
//
// x86-64 has no such split: `fs:0` is the variant-II ABI TCB's self-referential
// word, which ./`../thread/linux/tls.bit`'s `tlsPrepare` writes itself
// (`storeWord(tp, tp)`) and which static TLS writes identically, so the
// same load is the thread pointer under either runtime.
//
// `asm` — as opposed to `syscall`, `extern`, or a call through a function value
// — is legal in a `@nosplit` body; `sched.bit`'s `readSp` is the precedent, and
// every function that can reach the collector must be `@nosplit` (see
// `gcworld.bit`'s header). This file sorts between `gcmark` and `gcworld` in
// bytewise filename order (#1511), so the token is lowered before its only
// callers, and it references nothing — no helper, no module state, no import.

// This OS thread's identity, or 0 if no thread pointer is installed yet.
//
// The AArch64 payload, assembled with `clang -target aarch64-linux-gnu` and
// disassembled to confirm each word rather than hand-derived:
//
//   d53bd060   mrs  x0, tpidrro_el0    Darwin: the thread pointer. Linux: 0.
//   b5000040   cbnz x0, +8             non-zero -> that IS the answer
//   d53bd040   mrs  x0, tpidr_el0      Linux: the thread pointer.
//   927df000   and  x0, x0, #~7        Darwin packs a cpu id in the low bits
//
// The mask is harmless on Linux, where a thread pointer is at least 16-byte
// aligned by the TLS ABI, and load-bearing on Darwin, where TPIDRRO_EL0's low
// three bits carry the CPU number and would make one thread's token change
// under it every time the scheduler moved it between cores.
//
// x86-64:
//
//   64 48 8B 04 25 00 00 00 00   movq %fs:0, %rax
//
// ZERO IS A REAL ANSWER AND MUST STAY ONE. A thread that has not had its thread
// pointer installed — pre-boot, before `../thread/linux/tls.bit` runs — reads 0
// here, and every caller treats 0 as "no token, do not register". That is the
// same do-nothing this module's callers already do before `worldSetReady`, and it
// is the safe direction: an unregistered thread is not counted by the
// rendezvous, so no collection can proceed believing it has stopped.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WINDOWS BREAKS THE "ONE x64 LOAD COVERS EVERY OS" CLAIM ABOVE — DECIDED, NOT YET WIRED (#3410)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The x86-64 case above is correct for Linux and Darwin because both give a
// user thread a real FS base and both preserve whatever is written there
// (`storeWord(tp, tp)`, the same self-referential word `fs:0` reads back)
// across a context switch — the kernel's thread-switch path saves/restores it
// as architectural state. Windows does neither: user-mode FS is not a thread
// pointer at all on x64 (the TEB lives at GS, not FS), and #3410/#3391 measured
// on real hardware that a `WRFSBASE`-installed FS base is discarded across a
// deschedule regardless of who installs it or when — non-zero immediately
// before `parkSleepNs`/`CreateThread`, exactly `0000000000000000` after. So
// `runtime/root/windows/boot.bit`'s `winInstallMainThreadToken`/`winSetFsBase`
// and `runtime/thread/windows/spawn.bit`'s `tokenPool`/`winInstallThreadToken`
// rest on a false premise and cannot be made to work by re-issuing WRFSBASE
// more often — that still races with any deschedule between the write and the
// read, which is exactly the failure already measured.
//
// DECIDED MECHANISM FOR WINDOWS: read the TEB self-pointer at `gs:[0x30]`
// (`NT_TIB.Self`, the same field `NtCurrentTeb()`/`__readgsqword(0x30)` read
// throughout the Windows API surface — SEH's exception chain, `GetLastError`,
// FLS and TLS slots all rest on this exact value). It has every property this
// header already requires of a token and none of WRFSBASE's failure mode:
//
//   - OS-ALLOCATED, NOT USER-INSTALLED. The TEB exists before the thread's
//     first instruction runs; there is no install call, so there is no window
//     in which the token is missing and no ordering dependency on when a
//     boot/worker entry point happens to run it.
//   - SURVIVES SCHEDULING BY CONSTRUCTION, not by observation. The GS base is
//     what Windows itself switches on every context switch to keep the TEB
//     addressable at all (`GetLastError`, SEH, FLS, TLS all fail instantly if
//     it didn't) — this is the mechanism WRFSBASE tried to imitate ad hoc and
//     failed, not a second instance of the same risk.
//   - ONE INSTRUCTION, NO CALL. `gs:[0x30]` is an ordinary segment-relative
//     load (`65 48 8B 04 25 30 00 00 00` = `movq %gs:0x30, %rax`), the same
//     shape and cost as the `fs:0` load above, callable from this function's
//     `@nosplit` body with no `extern`/syscall.
//
// REJECTED: `TlsAlloc`/`TlsSetValue`/`TlsGetValue`. The slot value itself
// would survive scheduling (it lives inside the TEB), but getting it there
// needs an explicit `TlsSetValue` call on every thread's entry — the same
// "someone must remember to install it before the first read" ordering
// hazard WRFSBASE already has, and `TlsGetValue` is a real call through the
// import table, not an instruction, so it cannot satisfy this function's
// `@nosplit`, no-call contract the way `gs:[0x30]` does.
//
// WHAT THIS BUYS `runtime/root/windows/boot.bit`'s worker start (#3412): if
// `gcThreadToken` reads `gs:[0x30]` on Windows, `boot.bit`'s existing direct
// `CreateThread` at step 6 needs NO per-thread install call at all, for the
// boot thread or any worker — the token is valid from the thread's first
// instruction. `winInstallMainThreadToken`, `winSetFsBase`, and
// spawn.bit's `tokenPool`/`winInstallThreadToken` are dead code once this
// lands and should be deleted, not kept as a second, unused path.
//
// WIRED (#3420): this file's asm block used to have only two keys, `x64` and
// `arm64` (`compiler/parserasm.bit`'s `parseAsm`) — one x64 payload shared by
// every x86-64 OS, which is exactly the invariant Windows breaks. There is no
// OS axis to add to that grammar without a language change (out of scope
// here), so the payload becomes OS-specific the same way every other
// OS-specific low-level primitive in this runtime does: an `extern`
// declaration in this platform-free file, implemented per OS in a *paired*
// module's OS directory (`bit_rt_port_park_wake`/`wait` in
// `runtime/sched/sleep.bit`, implemented in
// `runtime/park/{linux,darwin,windows}/wait.bit` is the template —
// `scripts/g2archive.sh`'s `PLATFORM_PAIRS`/`WINDOWS_PAIRS` now list `gc`
// alongside it). `runtime/gc/{linux,darwin,windows}/gcthread.bit` hold the
// real bodies; this file only declares the extern and re-exposes it under its
// original Bit name so every existing call site in this module keeps working
// unchanged (`gcworldsync.bit`'s `mutatorSlot`, and
// `tests/stress/gcworld/gcworld.bit`, which imports `gcThreadToken` by name
// across the module boundary and therefore needs it to keep existing as an
// ordinary exported Bit function, not only as the raw pinned symbol).
//
// THE COST, STATED PLAINLY RATHER THAN LEFT IMPLICIT. Before this split,
// `gcThreadToken` was defined directly in this file and
// `compiler/opt.bit`'s `optimizeModule` inlined its ~9-byte x64 / 16-byte
// arm64 body straight into every caller in this module — confirmed by
// disassembling `bit_rt_port_gc_mutator_slot` before this change: the token
// read (`movq %fs:0x0, %rax`) appears inline in `mutatorSlot`'s own body, no
// `call`. A function defined in a SIBLING module — a separately compiled
// object per `scripts/g2archive.sh` — can never be inlined, because
// `optimizeModule` only inlines within one module's own index order. So this
// is now a real cross-object call on every target, Linux and Darwin included,
// not only Windows: `mutatorSlot` is reached from every `bit_rt_safepoint`
// site the compiler emits at a loop back edge (2147 of them in the shipped
// 0.1.12 image, per `currentMutator`'s header above), so the extra `call`+
// `ret` framing is paid on a genuinely hot path, not a cold one. There is no
// way to avoid this without either extending `parserasm`'s grammar with an OS
// axis (its own design decision, not this ticket's to make) or leaving the
// wrong-register bug this ticket exists to fix. What IS preserved exactly:
// the raw instruction bytes read out of `fs:0`/the `TPIDRRO_EL0`/`TPIDR_EL0`
// probe are byte-for-byte what they were before the split — see
// `runtime/gc/linux/gcthread.bit` and `runtime/gc/darwin/gcthread.bit`.
extern fn bit_rt_port_gc_thread_token(): int

// Thin re-export under the name every existing caller already uses — see the
// cost note immediately above for what this call now costs on every target.
// `@nosplit` to match every one of those callers' own requirement (a call to
// an `extern fn` needs no such marker on the callee per SPEC §11.7 — the
// permission attaches to the call site — but this wrapper is an ordinary Bit
// function, so it needs the attribute itself).
export @nosplit fn gcThreadToken(): int {
  return bit_rt_port_gc_thread_token()
}
